            0.075
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.092,
            0.169,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 1,
              "bbox": [
                0.079,
                0.092,
                0.169,
                0.18
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.09,
            0.341,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In-vitro Fertilisation (IVF) is a medical technique in which an egg and a sperm are combined outside the female"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.077,
            0.183,
            0.34,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "body, usually in a laboratory dish. The resultant fertilised egg is then implanted in the uterus to begin a possible pregnancy. A baby born through this method is commonly known as a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "test tube baby",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", although the process actually occurs in a culture dish, not in a test tube. India holds a significant place in the history of IVF. In 1978, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Subhash Mukhopadhyay",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of Kolkata pioneered India’s first test tube baby, Kanupriya Agarwal (nicknamed Durga), through experimental IVF work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.087,
            0.89,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The hormones bring about changes during puberty. The oviducts connect each ovary to the uterus. The uterus is a bag-like structure where a foetus develops. The uterus opens into the vagina through a narrow passage called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cervix",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.181,
            0.796,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.5.4 How are reproductive cells made?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.203,
            0.891,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The process of the formation of gametes is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "gametogenesis",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". It takes place in the testes and the ovaries. You have learnt in Activity 11.4 that gametes are formed by meiosis, in which the number of chromosomes is reduced to half. In humans, cells have 46 chromosomes, but sperm and eggs have only 23. This is important so that when they combine, the zygote has the same number of chromosomes as parents to form an individual. In males, gametogenesis results in the production of numerous tiny motile (moving), active sperm. In females, gametogenesis results in the formation of a single large egg. In sexual reproduction, male and female gametes differ greatly in size, number and structure (Table 11.5)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.227,
            0.466,
            0.885,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_caption",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.265,
                0.448,
                0.848,
                0.465
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 11.5: Structure, size, and number of male and female gametes"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.227,
                0.466,
                0.885,
                0.617
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_table_8.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Feature</td><td>Sperm</td><td>Egg</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Very small</td><td>Large</td></tr><tr><td>Number produced</td><td>Millions</td><td>Few</td></tr><tr><td>Stored nutrients</td><td>Absent</td><td>Present</td></tr><tr><td>Motility</td><td>Actively motile</td><td>Non-motile</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.624,
            0.888,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This asymmetry between the male and female gametes is seen across most of the animals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.668,
            0.738,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.5.5 What happens when a sperm meets an egg?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.689,
            0.89,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "At birth, a girl’s ovaries already have millions of immature eggs. From puberty onwards, usually one mature egg is released every month from one of the ovaries. This is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ovulation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Before ovulation, the uterus starts to prepare itself; the inner lining becomes thick. As the ovulation happens, the egg travels from the ovary to the oviduct. During sexual intercourse, millions of sperm enter through the vagina. They swim"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.047,
            0.795,
            0.352,
            0.885
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.047,
                0.795,
                0.352,
                0.885
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.281,
                0.889,
                0.333,
                0.903
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Zygote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.08,
            0.907,
            0.321,
            0.922
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 11.20: Process of fertilisation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.79,
            0.891,
            0.944
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "through the reproductive tract and may reach the egg in the oviduct. If a sperm encounters an egg and succeeds in fusing with it, a zygote is formed (Fig. 11.20). The inner lining of the uterus becomes thicker and richer in blood vessels. The zygote undergoes a series of mitotic divisions while travelling to the uterus, and implants into the inner lining of the uterus to receive nourishment for development. This implantation marks the beginning of pregnancy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
